LINUX FILE SYSTEM & CORE COMMANDS NOTES
================================================================================

PART 1: LINUX DIRECTORY HIERARCHY (WHAT LIVES WHERE)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/ (Root)
- The starting point of the entire Linux filesystem hierarchy.
- No drive letters (C:, D:) exist; all partitions and devices branch from here.

/home
- Workspaces for standard non-root users (e.g., /home/username).
- Houses user files, configurations, and personal data.

/root
- Dedicated home directory of the system administrator (root user).
- Kept on the root partition so admin access functions even if /home fails to mount.

/bin (Binaries)
- Core command-line executables required for basic operation and recovery.
- Examples: ls, cp, mv, rm, cat, mkdir.

/sbin (System Binaries)
- Administrative management utilities intended for the root user.
- Examples: fdisk, iptables, reboot, ip.

/usr (Unix System Resources)
- Secondary software library containing applications, utilities, and assets.
- Houses non-critical user-space programs (/usr/bin), documentation, and tools.

/lib
- Core shared libraries and kernel modules required by binaries in /bin and /sbin.
- Equivalent to .dll files in Windows; eliminates redundant code across programs.

/etc
- Central directory for system-wide and service configuration files.
- Examples: network settings, SSH configurations (/etc/ssh), DNS (/etc/resolv.conf).

/var (Variable)
- Dynamic, constantly changing runtime data.
- Stores system logs (/var/log), databases, print spools, and temporary caches.

/tmp (Temporary)
- Scratch storage for temporary files created by applications.
- Cleared periodically or completely wiped upon system reboot.

/dev (Devices)
- Hardware device nodes exposed as files ("everything is a file").
- Examples: /dev/sda (hard disk), /dev/null (data discard node).

/proc (Processes)
- In-memory virtual pseudo-filesystem created by the Linux kernel.
- Consumes 0 bytes on disk; provides live system stats, PID metrics, and CPU data.

/boot
- Static files required to boot the machine.
- Contains the compiled Linux kernel (vmlinuz), initramfs, and GRUB files.

/media & /mnt
- /media: Automatic mount directory for removable drives (USB, external disks).
- /mnt: Manual mount point for administrators attaching disks or network shares.


