PART 2: COMMAND COMPARISONS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. rmdir vs. rm -rf

Command: rmdir <directory>
- Purpose: Removes empty directories only.
- Mechanism: Makes an rmdir() system call. Fails immediately if any file, 
  subfolder, or hidden dotfile exists inside.
- Error when not empty: "Directory not empty".
- Safety: Completely safe against accidental data deletion.

Command: rm -rf <directory>
- Purpose: Recursively and forcefully deletes directories and all their contents.
- Flags:
  -r: Recursive traversal (descends into every child directory and file).
  -f: Force (ignores non-existent files and bypasses confirmation prompts).
- Mechanism: Unlinks every child file inode and removes directories bottom-up.
- Risk: Extremely dangerous if run with typos or unquoted shell variables 
  (e.g., rm -rf /path /to/dir will target root).


2. Vim Exit Commands: :wq vs. :q vs. :q! vs. :x

Command: :q (Quit)
- Action: Closes the current editor buffer and exits.
- Safety: Fails and throws "E37: No write since last change" if modifications 
  were made without saving.

Command: :wq (Write and Quit)
- Action: Saves (writes) memory buffer changes to disk, then exits Vim.
- Note: Always updates the file's modification timestamp (mtime), even if no 
  actual edits took place. Fails if opened without write permissions.

Command: :q! (Force Quit / Discard)
- Action: Exits immediately without saving.
- Discards all unsaved edits made during the current editing session.

Command: :x (Smart Exit)
- Action: Writes only if modifications exist, then exits.
- Difference from :wq: If you only viewed the file without changes, :x does not 
  update the file timestamp.


