ABSOLUTE VS. RELATIVE PATHS IN LINUX
================================================================================

1. CORE DEFINITIONS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Absolute Path:
- Specifies the exact, full address of a file or directory starting from the root (/).
- Always begins with a forward slash (/).
- Works identically no matter where you are currently standing in the filesystem.
- Analogy: A complete postal address with country, city, street, and house number.

Relative Path:
- Specifies the path in relation to your current working directory (pwd).
- Never begins with a forward slash (/).
- Meaning and target change whenever you switch directories (cd).
- Analogy: Directions relative to where you stand ("walk two doors down").


2. KEY RELATIVE PATH SYMBOLS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
.  (Single Dot)
- Refers to the CURRENT directory you are in right now.
- Example: ./script.sh tells the shell to run the script inside the current folder
  instead of searching the system $PATH.

.. (Double Dot)
- Refers to the PARENT directory (one level up the tree).
- Example: cd .. moves one step up.
- Example: ../../etc moves two steps up, then into the etc folder.


3. VISUAL DIRECTORY TREE EXAMPLE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Assume this directory layout on disk:

/ (root)
├── var/
│   └── log/
│       └── app.log
└── home/
    └── user/
        ├── movies/
        │   └── clip.mp4
        └── projects/           <-- [CURRENT WORKING DIRECTORY: pwd]
            ├── script.sh
            └── config.json


Scenario A: Run script.sh (located inside current directory)
- Absolute: /home/user/projects/script.sh
- Relative: ./script.sh

Scenario B: Read app.log (located across the filesystem in /var/log)
- Absolute: /var/log/app.log
- Relative: ../../../var/log/app.log

Scenario C: Switch into sibling directory 'movies'
- Absolute: cd /home/user/movies
- Relative: cd ../movies

Scenario D: Create a folder called 'data' inside current directory
- Absolute: mkdir /home/user/projects/data
- Relative: mkdir data   (or mkdir ./data)


4. UNDERLYING MECHANISM: HOW LINUX RESOLVES PATHS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Every process tracks two directory pointers in kernel memory (fs_struct):
  1. root: Base of the process namespace (normally /).
  2. pwd: The current working directory inode.

- Path resolution rule:
  - If the path starts with '/', the kernel starts resolution at root.
  - If the path starts with anything else, resolution starts at pwd.


5. WHEN THINGS BREAK (COMMON TRAPS & FIXES)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Trap 1: Broken Cron Jobs & systemd Services
- Problem: A script containing "cat config.json" runs fine manually, but fails
  with "No such file or directory" when triggered by Cron.
- Cause: Cron runs from /root or /home/user by default, not your project folder.
- Fix: Always use absolute paths in automation, or dynamically resolve the path:
  SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
  cat "$SCRIPT_DIR/config.json"

Trap 2: Broken Symlinks
- Problem: Moving a symbolic link or copying files across servers breaks the link.
- Cause: Absolute symlinks point to a hardcoded path that may not exist in the new
  environment.
- Fix: Use relative symlinks inside project repositories so they remain portable:
  ln -s ../shared/config.json ./config.json


6. QUICK CHEAT SHEET
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Path Type     Starts With?       Depends on pwd?   Best Used For
-----------   ----------------   ---------------   -----------------------------
Absolute      Always starts /    No                Scripts, Cron, systemd, IaC
Relative      Names, ., or ..    Yes               Interactive terminal work

Useful Commands:
- pwd                  -> Prints current absolute working directory
- realpath <path>      -> Converts any relative path into its absolute path
================================================================================
• . (Single Dot): Represents your current directory. It tells the system, "look right here."
• .. (Double Dot): Represents the parent directory. It moves you one level up in the folder structure.
• ~ (Tilde): Represents your home directory (e.g., /home/username). It is a quick shortcut to your personal files from anywhere in the system.
• - (Dash): Represents the previous working directory. It acts like a "back" button, toggling you back to the folder you were in just before your last cd command.
