Quick Summary
basename = "What is the name of this file?"

dirname = "Where does this file live?"

Example
Take this path: /home/user/documents/notes.txt

basename:

Bash
basename /home/user/documents/notes.txt
Output: notes.txt

dirname:

Bash
dirname /home/user/documents/notes.txt
Output: /home/user/documents

============================================================================

Summary Rule of Thumb
Use dirname / basename when you need to split or modify path names (e.g., getting a file's name, changing an extension, or finding its immediate parent folder).

Use realpath / readlink when you need to trace where a symlink actually points or convert messy paths (../..) into a single, clean absolute path.

